  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1228 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nSfc high pressure over the Ohio Valley is allowing for unseasonably \ncool and pleasant conditions today. Dewpoints have mixed down into \nthe 50s due to northeasterly flow, with a deep dry layer around\n800 mb. The break from the heat and humidity will not last for \nlong as southerly return flow begins to advect in moisture on \nFriday. Rain chances also begin to increase with a 500 mb \nshortwave trough approaching the FA by Friday evening. Most of \nFriday outside of a few sprinkles towards SEMO likely remains \ndry, but the boundary layer eventually saturates Friday night \nsupporting scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms. The \nlatest CAMs are currently struggling with a decaying complex of \nelevated storms early Saturday morning due to the location of a \nLLJ, but it does appear SEMO may be more favored for the \npotential of heavier rainfall. QPF currently ranges from one \ntenth to a half inch of rainfall, but locally higher amounts are\npossible with rain chances peaking at a 50-60% Saturday \nmorning. \n\nA ridge of high pressure centered over the western CONUS builds more \ninto the FA on Sunday with a return to dry conditions. Latest model \nruns favor higher height rises, meaning the risk for storms riding \nalong the ridge may end up staying north and east of the FA between \nSunday and Tuesday. Still a non-zero chance something eventually \nsneaks down, but the current NBM forecast does not reflect this. \nHowever, the lack of rain means higher confidence in heat headlines \nmaking a return with the western half pushing a heat index of 105 by \nSunday. Most of the FA likely will be near heat advisory\ncriteria during the first half of next week.",
